{:schema 1, :ic09-steps [{:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 3.596416, :dedup-ratio 1.0, :grouped-faster-by 0.23536113152940338, :grouped-ms 15.280416, :bindings-out 882, :distinct-patterns 1, :bindings-in 1, :clause [?person "knows" ?f1], :step 0} {:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 212.402125, :dedup-ratio 1.0, :grouped-faster-by 1.1814560831079959, :grouped-ms 179.779958, :bindings-out 55335, :distinct-patterns 882, :bindings-in 882, :clause [?f1 "knows" ?f2], :step 1} {:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 526.78125, :dedup-ratio 6.806273062730627, :grouped-faster-by 0.6533501461308433, :grouped-ms 806.277083, :bindings-out 87052, :distinct-patterns 8130, :bindings-in 55335, :clause [?msg "hasCreator" ?f2], :step 2} {:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 445.2475, :dedup-ratio 12.92915490865884, :grouped-faster-by 1.2905224326448215, :grouped-ms 345.013375, :bindings-out 87052, :distinct-patterns 6733, :bindings-in 87052, :clause [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date], :step 3}], :start-person "17592186053137", :adr "ADR-2608021000 §6-5-1 -- diagnose IC02 by measuring, after reasoning about it produced a fix that made it worse", :caveats ["Both strategies are reimplemented here from the same primitives arrangement uses (substitute / unify-positional are private there). :answers-equal per step is what keeps that honest -- if a step ever reports false, this harness is measuring something other than the engine." "Single run per step on one start person. This is a diagnosis, not a benchmark; the percentiles are in the LDBC receipts." "The two strategies alternate which one runs first across steps, so neither systematically benefits from the other having warmed the same rows."], :ic02-steps [{:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 2.419584, :dedup-ratio 1.0, :grouped-faster-by 0.917479394875186, :grouped-ms 2.637208, :bindings-out 882, :distinct-patterns 1, :bindings-in 1, :clause [?person "knows" ?f], :step 0} {:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 4.026459, :dedup-ratio 1.0, :grouped-faster-by 0.8218940600122474, :grouped-ms 4.899, :bindings-out 910, :distinct-patterns 882, :bindings-in 882, :clause [?msg "hasCreator" ?f], :step 1} {:answers-equal true, :per-binding-ms 4.189708, :dedup-ratio 1.0, :grouped-faster-by 0.7014558315718981, :grouped-ms 5.972875, :bindings-out 910, :distinct-patterns 910, :bindings-in 910, :clause [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date], :step 2}], :receipt/type :ic02-diagnosis, :reading "Per step: :dedup-ratio is how much grouping COULD win (bindings per distinct pattern) and :grouped-faster-by is how much it DID. A step with ratio 1.0 and grouped-faster-by below 1.0 is paying for the group-by and collecting nothing -- which is the shape IC02 was accused of having. If IC02's steps do not look like that, the regression is somewhere else and the first explanation was wrong for a second reason.", :implementation "kotobase-peer.ic02-diag", :dataset {:subset-posts 3000, :quads 409877, :knows-quads 361246, :messages 7355}
 :findings
 ["IC02: every step has :dedup-ratio 1.0 -- one distinct substituted pattern per binding, at all three steps -- and grouping is slower at every one of them (0.92x, 0.82x, 0.70x). The MECHANISM in my original explanation was right: IC02 pays for the group-by and has nothing to collect."
  "But IC02's whole join is ~13 ms here, against the 80-102 ms the LDBC harness measures for IC02 end to end. The join is not where IC02's time is. Two of its clauses probe as full 7,355-row scans in datalog-query-plan, and that is the term that matches the magnitude. IC02 is a :query-statistics problem, not a join problem -- the item that was deprioritised."
  "IC09: the step with real dedup available (55,335 bindings -> 8,130 distinct, 6.81x) is STILL slower grouped (0.65x). Grouping 55,335 three-element vector keys costs more than the 47,000 keyed scans it avoids, because a keyed scan into an index is already cheap. Only the last step (12.93x dedup) pays off, at 1.29x."]
 :discrepancy-not-resolved
 {:what "This harness runs IC09's four join steps per-binding in ~1.19 s. bench/results/2026-08-01-ic09-diagnosis.edn measured the real engine's per-binding path at 35.6 s on the same data and machine class. Thirty-fold, unexplained."
  :why-it-matters "The ~11.5x I attributed to join batching (2026-08-02-ic09-after-hash-join.edn) rests on that 35.6 s baseline. If the baseline was not what these four steps cost, the attribution is not safe. I am not withdrawing the number, and I am not defending it either -- it is in question until the discrepancy is closed."
  :candidate-causes ["This bench reimplements substitute/unify-positional (they are private in arrangement); the reimplementation may not be faithful in cost even though :answers-equal is true at every step."
                     "The real path goes through scan*, which calls satisfies? per scan; this bench calls arrangement.query/query directly."
                     "datalog/q does work outside these four steps -- fixpoint setup, clause safety checks, projection -- that this bench does not."]
  :next-measurement "Instrument the REAL join-clause with counters and timers rather than reimplementing it. Reimplementing was the wrong instrument for this question: it answers what the strategies cost, not what the engine does."}
 :what-this-changes
 ["The group-by skip that was reverted has a real basis after all -- but from THIS measurement (in-process, interleaved, no host variance), not from the LDBC ratio that motivated the first attempt. It is not being re-landed on the strength of one bench that also produced an unexplained 30x."
  "IC02's next step is :query-statistics, not the join."
  "The batching win needs re-establishing against the real engine before it is quoted again."]
 :reading-notes
 [":answers-equal is true at every step, so the two strategies agree; that does not prove either matches the engine's cost."
